{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Behold, I come to you, etc.</b> Perhaps it intends to say to him that since HaShem was accustomed to speak with him face to face, therefore He said to him that He would speak with him in a different manner, namely, <b>in the thickness of the cloud</b>, and the speech would not be as spiritual as the previous custom. And He gave the reason: <b>in order that the people may hear</b> when He speaks with Moses — for if He were to speak in the original manner, through the illuminating speculum, there would be no power in Israel to hear the words on account of their sheer spirituality, and HaShem desired that the utterances be made audible to Israel. And He said to him that besides this purpose — that the people hear the commandment from the mouth of the Creator, which constitutes a strengthening of faith in their hearts — there is an additional benefit: that they will believe in Moses forever regarding all other commandments when he commands them by the word of HaShem. And HaShem intended by this as well to appease Moses, so that he would not take offense at the diminishment of his honor from the usual manner of speech." "<b>They will believe forever.</b> The meaning of ״forever״ is: for the generations to come they will believe in your intermediacy regarding all the prophets, along the lines of what Rambam wrote in chapter 8 of Hilkhot Yesodei ha-Torah, and this is his language: any prophet who arises after Moses our Master — we do not believe in him because of the sign, etc., but rather because of the commandment that Moses commanded in the Torah and said, ״if he gives you a sign, you shall heed him״ — end quote. And he wrote further there that Moses' faith was not on account of the wonders, etc., but rather because they heard HaShem speaking with him, and they are witnesses to the truth of his prophecy. And on the basis of these words we can interpret <b>and also in you they will believe forever</b> — the reason it says ״forever״ alludes as well to the other prophets who come after him forever: when a prophet also comes to them, sent by Me, they will affirm his prophecy, for the signs he performs do not authenticate prophecy except by the command of HaShem concerning their faith; and without this distinction, even the one commanded by this commandment remains subject to doubt when his faith rests upon signs alone, apart from when the people hear Him speaking with him." "Yet one must give attention to Rambam's words: how can it be correct to say that they did not believe in Moses, when it is already stated (Exodus 14:31) ״וַיַּאֲמִינוּ בַה׳ וּבְמֹשֶׁה״ etc.? For his wonders and signs are extraordinary, and all the more so the miracle of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds — it is an overwhelming thing, and through it they would affirm and compel faith. It is true that regarding other prophets, since there is no need for them to perform signs as mighty and great as the signs of Moses, therefore we need to strengthen faith in their prophecy on the basis of HaShem's word that commanded us to heed them — unlike Moses. And as for what Rambam wrote there, that the signs Moses performed were not so that they would believe in him but rather for the needs of Israel, etc., as stated there — in the end the truth is heard of itself, that Moses is true." "<b>But it appears</b> that the faith that Israel believed in Moses was that he was the servant of HaShem and HaShem desired him and fulfilled his prayer and will, but they did not believe that HaShem spoke with him. For there is a view among the sages of the nations who prove with many demonstrations according to their philosophies that it is absolutely impossible for HaShem to speak with a human being, yet these would concede that HaShem hears and answers and loves His beloved and fulfills their will. And since this view exists in the world, we may say that although Israel believed in Moses His servant — meaning that he is the servant of HaShem and HaShem hears his prayer and does his desire and will — but from this alone they would not concede that HaShem speaks, and the concern of the philosophers of the nations still persists. Therefore HaShem said to him, <b>in order that the people may hear when I speak with you</b> — and through this they would concede that HaShem speaks with a human being." "And His saying <b>and also in you they will believe</b> — meaning, through your intermediacy they will believe in the prophet whom I will establish for them in your stead in every generation forever, for the claim of this philosophical view will be refuted among them. Or it may intend His saying <b>in you</b> to mean: through the intermediacy of your prophecy — once they affirm it, when you command them that they shall heed a prophet on the basis of a sign, they will believe forever in every prophet; but without the authentication of your prophecy, the sign would not avail to believe in the prophet, as Rambam wrote. After I wrote this, I found a baraita in the Mekhilta, and this is their language: ״also in you״ — also in the prophets who come after you — end quote. And these are our very words, and with what I have explained you will understand the intent of their words." "And behold, when the people heard the words of HaShem, they all justified together that HaShem speaks with man and he lives, and as they declared with their mouth and said (Deuteronomy 5:21): ״הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה רָאִינוּ כִּי יְדַבֵּר ה׳ עִם הָאָדָם וָחָי״, and they are witnesses of HaShem to the matter, and the opinion of the philosophers is disproved with clear vision. And they also recognized the greatness of the prophet Moses — in that HaShem elevated him in his prophecy — as it is written (Exodus 19:19): ״מֹשֶׁה יְדַבֵּר וְהָאֱלֹהִים יַעֲנֶנּוּ בְקוֹל״, and as our Sages of blessed memory expounded the verse (Mekhilta, Yitro); their words may be examined there." "<b>And Moses reported, etc.</b> Perhaps he reported that they agreed to hear the words of HaShem from His mouth, as HaShem said: ״בַּעֲבוּר יִשְׁמַע הָעָם״ etc., or that no further sign is needed to believe in Moses, for they already believed in Moses that he is a prophet. And the intent of the reporting is merely to give merit to Israel alone, and according to their words of blessed memory (Mekhilta) who said that the reporting is that Israel said: our will is to hear from the mouth of HaShem; and according to the one who said to see our King, the language of ״reporting״ is precise, for these are difficult things — that a servant should seek to hear the voice of his Elohim or to see His glory, blessed be He. And although we find that HaShem said ״בַּעֲבוּר יִשְׁמַע הָעָם״, He did not say that He would speak with them, but rather that when He speaks with Moses they would hear; yet their request was that HaShem speak with them. And the reason for the reporting, even though nothing is hidden from Him, is to respond to their request. And we find that He responded immediately: ״לֵךְ אֶל הָעָם וְקִדַּשְׁתָּם״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:36.056232123Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}